Falsifies `intrusive_queue::verification::removing_any_node_keeps_the_rest_in_order`.

The defect: unlinking a node stops moving `tail` back when the node was the last one. The bridge
write over the gap is right and the order of the nodes left behind is right, so nothing looks
wrong until the next push, which links the new node behind the node that has already left.

Expected to fail: the harness's check that the node pushed after the removal comes out, whenever
the removed node was the tail. With one node queued, `head` is `None` after the removal, the push
takes the `Some` arm on the stale tail, and the queue reports itself empty while the pushed node
is lost.

diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -488,9 +488,6 @@
                 Some(previous) => write_link(previous, next),
                 None => self.head = next,
             }
-            if next.is_none() {
-                self.tail = previous;
-            }
             write_link(node, None);
         }
         // Wrapping: `node` was on the queue, so `len >= 1`, as in `pop_front`.
